## How is Amazon search optimisation different from Google SEO?

Amazon search optimisation differs from Google SEO because Amazon's search exists to sell products. It favours listings that match the search term and convert. Google ranks pages by how well they answer a search. Understand the difference before you touch a listing.

{% panels %}
{% panel eyebrow="Google" title="Ranks pages that answer a search" %}
- Google describes relevance, content quality, usability and context as ranking inputs
- Rewards long-tail informational queries and content clusters
- Keywords live in page copy, titles and headings across a site
{% /panel %}
{% panel eyebrow="Amazon" title="Ranks products that match and convert" %}
- Amazon describes its ranking in terms of relevance to the search and how well the product performs
- Rewards transactional search terms with clear purchase intent
- Keywords live in the title, bullet points and backend search terms
{% /panel %}
{% /panels %}

The industry calls Amazon's ranking system A9 or A10. Amazon's own guidance centres on relevance and product performance. Backlinks and page speed are staples of Google SEO and are not part of that description. In my audits I treat conversion, fulfilment speed and review ratings as the levers a seller can influence. Keyword research and intent mapping transfer from Google SEO. Titles, bullets and backend terms follow Amazon's own format rules. Support beyond Amazon is covered by my [ecommerce SEO consultant](/services/ecommerce-seo-consultant/) service.

## Which Amazon listing mistakes do I check for first?

I check titles, backend search terms, images, A+ Content and reviews first in any Amazon listing audit. These are the five mistakes I look for first.

**Keyword-stuffed titles.** Sellers cram every possible keyword into the product title. The result reads as spam and can hurt click-through rate. Amazon does read title keywords. It also measures whether shoppers click and buy. A title that reads like a keyword dump puts shoppers off.

**Ignoring backend search terms.** Amazon gives you a hidden backend search terms field for keywords that do not appear in the visible listing. Many sellers leave it blank, duplicate title keywords or waste characters on brand names. Used properly it expands keyword coverage without cluttering the listing.

**Poor product images.** Amazon is a visual platform. Listings with low-quality images, too few images or no image of the product in use often convert worse. The main image decides whether someone clicks. The gallery decides whether they buy.

**No A+ Content.** Brand Registry gives you access to A+ Content. Listings without it miss a chance to convert. A+ Content adds comparison charts, lifestyle imagery and feature breakdowns below the fold.

**Neglecting reviews.** Review count and rating affect clicks and conversions. Amazon's ranking rewards products that sell. Sellers without a review strategy inside Amazon's terms of service are at a disadvantage.

